    source: "Opening bars imported from the Mutopia Project (vordeinenT.ly), Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 2.5, converted via python-ly + music21/xml2abc. Bars 1-4, imported 2026-08-08 (round 42). Bars 2-3 of the merged alto+tenor staff measured `over` (an `8.0/4.0` doubling) through rounds 39-41 -- round 39 suspected a P30 frame-nesting gap, round 41 ruled that out and traced it instead to the pedal's own `R1*3` multi-bar rest: `Mediator.scale_rest` (P24) builds bar 1 of a multiplied rest through the normal `check_current_note` path, which stamps a `<staff>` on it, but builds every later copy directly, skipping that call -- so bars 2 and 3 of the pedal's rest went out staff-less. With the tenor sharing that same voice number (an unrelated numbering coincidence between P30's frame-local voice count and the next `\\context Staff`'s own), music21 had nothing but the tenor's voice number to route those staff-less rests by when splitting the PianoStaff's single 3-staff `<part>` back into one stream per staff, and folded them into the MIDDLE of tenor's bars 2 and 3 instead of the pedal's own. Fixed as P47 (`tools/vendor/ly_musicxml_patch.py`, extends P24's `scale_rest`): capture `self.staff` once before the loop and stamp it on every copy, exactly as the first bar already gets. Bar 1 hand-verified pitch-for-pitch against the .ly for all three staves (dessus/choral rests through the chorale's late entrance; alto and tenor's opening phrases; pedal's three-bar rest). Whole score passed all four batch_import.py gates (duration, census, collapsed-chord, discarded-notes); bars 44-45's nested `\\`-voice split (P46, round 41) also re-verified pitch-for-pitch. The same P47 fix changed the shipped ABC of ten already-imported pieces that also carry a multi-bar rest sharing a voice number with a sibling staff (BWV 649, 663, 670, 671, 648, 680, 723, Ad Cœnam 3e/Iste Confessor 2e verset, Dandrieu's Offertoire) -- each moved from a spurious extra all-but-silent voice fragmenting a real voice's content to that content sitting correctly in one continuous stream (confirmed structurally for BWV 649 and BWV 723 by diffing before/after ABC: the phantom voice disappears and its notes reappear inline, none lost). Not yet individually re-verified pitch-for-pitch and re-pasted here -- queued for a follow-up round.",
